{"kind":"symbol","metadata":{"fragments":[{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"CartesianProduct","kind":"identifier"}],"symbolKind":"struct","navigatorTitle":[{"text":"CartesianProduct","kind":"identifier"}],"modules":[{"name":"GraphProducts"}],"remoteSource":{"url":"https:\/\/github.com\/Tyler-Keith-Thompson\/Grafluent\/blob\/main\/Sources\/GraphProducts\/CartesianProduct.swift#L49","fileName":"CartesianProduct.swift"},"externalID":"s:13GraphProducts16CartesianProductV","title":"CartesianProduct","role":"symbol","roleHeading":"Structure"},"primaryContentSections":[{"kind":"declarations","declarations":[{"tokens":[{"text":"@frozen","kind":"attribute"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"CartesianProduct","kind":"identifier"},{"text":"<","kind":"text"},{"text":"First","kind":"genericParameter"},{"text":", ","kind":"text"},{"text":"Second","kind":"genericParameter"},{"text":">","kind":"text"}],"platforms":["Linux"],"languages":["swift"]}]},{"kind":"content","content":[{"anchor":"overview","type":"heading","level":2,"text":"Overview"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"strong","inlineContent":[{"text":"Vertices","type":"text"}]},{"type":"text","text":" are the pairs "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Pair(g, h)"},{"type":"text","text":". Vertex (g, h) has index "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"index(g)·|V(H)| + index(h)"},{"type":"text","text":","},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"row-major, the numbering of NamedGraphs’ grids, so "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"vertex(atIndex:)"},{"type":"text","text":" costs one division and"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"vertexIndex(of:)"},{"type":"text","text":" one lookup in each factor."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"inlineContent":[{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Rows"}],"type":"strong"},{"text":" list the G-moves first, ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"(g′, h)"},{"text":" for each entry g′ of G’s row of g, then the H-moves","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"(g, h′)"},{"text":". They are computed from the factors’ index rows and never hash. A factor’s self-loop","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"gives a product self-loop, listed twice in an undirected row as the ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Graph"},{"text":" law asks.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"strong","inlineContent":[{"text":"Edges","type":"text"}]},{"type":"text","text":" are named by the factor edge and the factor vertex that generate them:"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":".first(edge: e, vertexIndex: h)"},{"type":"text","text":" is G’s edge "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"e"},{"type":"text","text":" at H’s vertex index "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"h"},{"type":"text","text":", and"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":".second(vertexIndex: g, edge: e)"},{"type":"text","text":" is H’s edge "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"e"},{"type":"text","text":" at G’s vertex index "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"g"},{"type":"text","text":". "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"edges"},{"type":"text","text":" lists every"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":".first"},{"type":"text","text":" edge, G-edge outer, then every "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":".second"},{"type":"text","text":" edge, G-vertex outer. When both factors have"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"edge indices, so does the product: "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"index(e)·|V(H)| + h"},{"type":"text","text":", then "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"m_G·|V(H)| + g·m_H + index(e)"},{"type":"text","text":"."},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"Products of multigraphs and pseudographs are defined edge by edge, so "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"edgeCount"},{"type":"text","text":", the rows and"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"the degrees always agree."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"To materialize it, pass it to a representation’s initializer. Products are "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Sendable"},{"type":"text","text":" when both"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"factors are."}],"type":"paragraph"}]}],"relationshipsSections":[{"identifiers":["doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/BidirectionalDirectedGraph","doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/DirectedGraph","doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/Graph","doc:\/\/GraphProducts\/s8CopyableP","doc:\/\/GraphProducts\/s9EscapableP","doc:\/\/GraphProducts\/s8SendableP","doc:\/\/GraphProducts\/s16SendableMetatypeP"],"kind":"relationships","title":"Conforms To","type":"conformsTo"}],"identifier":{"url":"doc:\/\/GraphProducts\/documentation\/GraphProducts\/CartesianProduct","interfaceLanguage":"swift"},"schemaVersion":{"minor":3,"major":0,"patch":0},"variants":[{"paths":["\/documentation\/graphproducts\/cartesianproduct"],"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}]}],"sections":[],"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/GraphProducts\/documentation\/GraphProducts"]]},"topicSections":[{"generated":true,"anchor":"Structures","identifiers":["doc:\/\/GraphProducts\/documentation\/GraphProducts\/CartesianProduct\/DirectedEdges","doc:\/\/GraphProducts\/documentation\/GraphProducts\/CartesianProduct\/EdgeIndexRow","doc:\/\/GraphProducts\/documentation\/GraphProducts\/CartesianProduct\/EdgeRow","doc:\/\/GraphProducts\/documentation\/GraphProducts\/CartesianProduct\/IndexRow","doc:\/\/GraphProducts\/documentation\/GraphProducts\/CartesianProduct\/UndirectedEdges"],"title":"Structures"},{"generated":true,"anchor":"Instance-Properties","identifiers":["doc:\/\/GraphProducts\/documentation\/GraphProducts\/CartesianProduct\/first","doc:\/\/GraphProducts\/documentation\/GraphProducts\/CartesianProduct\/second"],"title":"Instance Properties"},{"generated":true,"anchor":"Enumerations","identifier